We say that a polynomial is good if it has only complex roots. Note that for any real polynomial, its roots are completely determined by its irreducible factors over the real numbers.  Importantly, a polynomial factor over the reals is either irreducible over the reals or can be factored into the product of two real linear factors.  Therefore, we will only consider irreducible real polynomials when counting the number of good polynomials.
Let $P(x) = ax^3+bx^2+cx+d$ be a monic cubic polynomial. The roots of $P(x)$ are $\rho_1,$ $\rho_2,$ and $\rho_3,$ which are either real or come in pairs of complex conjugates.
Let $\rho_1=\rho_2=\rho_3 = r$ be the case in which $P(x)$ has a triple root $r$.

>From Vieta's, when $P(x)$ has a triple root $r$, we have $r=-\frac{b}{3 a}$. Because we're searching for the number of good polynomials, it follows that if $P(x)$ has only real roots, then it must be the case that $-\frac{b}{3a}$ is real. If $P(x)$ does not have any real roots, then it must be the case that $-\frac{b}{3a}$ is not real.

