Since we don't know the normal vector directly, but we know we can obtain it using the cross product of the direction vectors of the lines.

## Step 3: To get the cross product, we will compute the determinant.
To get the cross product of \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ k \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} and \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}, we will compute the determinant of \begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ 3 & k & 7 \\ 1 & 4 & 7 \end{pmatrix}.

## Step 4: Here is the computation of the determinant to get the cross product.
The cross product is \begin{pmatrix} (k \cdot 7) - (7 \cdot 4) \\ (7 \cdot 1) - (3 \cdot 7) \\ (3 \cdot 4) - (k \cdot 1) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 7k - 28 \\ 7 - 21 \\ 12 - k \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 7k - 28 \\ -14 \\ 12 - k \end{pmatrix}.

